<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     PROBLEM — blunt text, no cards
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="problem">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="problem__label">The problem</p>
    <div class="problem__grid">

      <div class="problem__statement">
        Python&rsquo;s default tooling is locked inside one proprietary VS Code extension. Python deserves a fully open-source language server that does <em>everything</em> &mdash; in <em>every</em> editor.
      </div>

      <ul class="problem__bullets">
        <li>
          <span class="problem__num">01</span>
          <span><strong>The most-used Python tooling is locked to one editor.</strong> The most popular Python extension is proprietary, and its richest features don&rsquo;t leave VS Code. Basilisk is MIT/Apache-2.0 and runs the same everywhere &mdash; VS Code, Cursor, Windsurf, Zed, Neovim &mdash; so the editor is your choice, not a constraint.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="problem__num">02</span>
          <span><strong>Pyright is only a type checker.</strong> No autocomplete, no hover, no refactoring &mdash; not without Pylance wrapping it. Basilisk is a complete LSP: diagnostics, completions, go-to-definition, inlay hints, and more.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="problem__num">03</span>
          <span><strong>Every type checker defaults to permissive.</strong> You get real checking only where you add <code>--strict</code>. Basilisk checks to the Python typing spec out of the box &mdash; and lets you turn strictness up from there.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="problem__num">04</span>
          <span><strong>Node.js is not a Python dependency.</strong> Pyright requires Node.js. Basilisk is a single Rust binary &mdash; no runtime, no npm, instant startup.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     FEATURES — alternating split rows
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="features">

  <div class="feature-row">
    <div class="feature-row__text">
      <p class="feature-row__num">01 &mdash; Conformance first</p>
      <h2 class="feature-row__title">Conformant by default.<br>Strict to taste.</h2>
      <p class="feature-row__body">
        Basilisk checks to the Python typing spec out of the box &mdash; the same rules the
        official conformance suite grades. Stricter checks are Basilisk&rsquo;s own and entirely
        opt-in: require an annotation on every parameter and return, flag redundant annotations,
        nudge for <code>@override</code>. The spec treats unannotated code as <em>inferred</em>,
        not an error, so these stay off by default &mdash; switch them on through config, per-file
        or per-path, when you want more than the spec. And one-click enrichment fixes add the
        missing types <em>for</em> you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-row__visual">
      <div class="stat-block">
        <span class="stat-block__number">{{ ruleStats.total }}</span>
        <span class="stat-block__label">rules, every one configurable</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-row">
    <div class="feature-row__visual">
      <div class="stat-block">
        <span class="stat-block__number">10<span class="stat-block__unit">/10</span></span>
        <span class="stat-block__label">benchmarked rules beat Pyright</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-row__text">
      <p class="feature-row__num">02 &mdash; Measured, not promised</p>
      <h2 class="feature-row__title">Faster than Pyright.<br>Every rule we benchmark.</h2>
      <p class="feature-row__body">
        Every release runs <code>make bench</code> &mdash; hyperfine wall-clock against Pyright,
        mypy, ty, and Pyrefly on identical fixtures, with the numbers committed to the repo. On
        the rules we benchmark, Basilisk&rsquo;s cold full-file checks beat Pyright outright. And
        because re-checks are incremental &mdash; only the edited file and its importers are
        re-analyzed &mdash; warm edits do even less work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-row">
    <div class="feature-row__text">
      <p class="feature-row__num">03 &mdash; Quick fixes &amp; refactoring</p>
      <h2 class="feature-row__title">Lightbulb-ready.<br>Every diagnostic.</h2>
      <p class="feature-row__body">
        Many diagnostics ship with a one-keystroke fix &mdash; add the missing annotation,
        modernise <code>Optional[X]</code> to <code>X | None</code>, convert to an f-string.
        Plus a full refactoring suite: extract, inline, change signature, and move symbol.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-row__visual">
      <div class="code-window" style="width:100%;max-width:400px">
        <div class="code-window__chrome">
          <span class="code-window__dot code-window__dot--close"></span>
          <span class="code-window__dot code-window__dot--min"></span>
          <span class="code-window__dot code-window__dot--expand"></span>
          <span class="code-window__filename">quick-fix.py</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-window__body">
<pre><span class="tok-cmt"># 💡 Quick Fix available</span>
<span class="tok-kw">def</span> <span class="tok-fn">process</span>(data) <span class="tok-cmt"># BSK-E0001</span>

<span class="tok-cmt"># → Add type annotation</span>
<span class="tok-kw">def</span> <span class="tok-fn">process</span>(data<span class="tok-type">: str</span>) <span class="tok-kw">-></span> <span class="tok-type">str</span>:
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> data.upper()</pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-row">
    <div class="feature-row__visual">
      <div class="stat-block">
        <span class="stat-block__number">{{ conformance.scorePct }}<span class="stat-block__unit">%</span></span>
        <span class="stat-block__label">PEP conformance score</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-row__text">
      <p class="feature-row__num">04 &mdash; PEP conformance</p>
      <h2 class="feature-row__title">{{ conformance.pass }} of {{ conformance.total }} tests passing ({{ conformance.scorePct }}%).<br>Every conformance rule enabled.</h2>
      <p class="feature-row__body">
        Scored by the <a href="https://github.com/python/typing/blob/main/conformance/results/results.html">official Python typing conformance suite</a>, graded against <a href="https://github.com/python/typing/tree/{{ conformance.pinnedRef }}/conformance">commit <code>{{ conformance.pinnedRefShort }}</code></a> &mdash; the exact suite version we target &mdash;
        the same published harness that grades Pyright, mypy, and Pyrefly, whose
        current scores live in that results table.
        And we stay in lock step with that repository: every checker build and every release re-pulls the latest suite from <code>python/typing@main</code> and requires a perfect score, so an upstream test we fail blocks merge and release until we conform.
        We run it on the real binary with every conformance rule enabled and nothing disabled: {{ conformance.categoriesPass100 }} of {{ conformance.categoriesTotal }} categories pass at 100%, with {{ conformance.fp }} false positives and {{ conformance.missed }} missed required errors. Basilisk&rsquo;s opt-in rules never run during conformance, so they can&rsquo;t pad the number. <a href="/docs/conformance/">How we measure this &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-row">
    <div class="feature-row__visual">
      <div class="code-window" style="width:100%;max-width:400px">
        <div class="code-window__chrome">
          <span class="code-window__dot code-window__dot--close"></span>
          <span class="code-window__dot code-window__dot--min"></span>
          <span class="code-window__dot code-window__dot--expand"></span>
          <span class="code-window__filename">Basilisk Output</span>
        </div>
        <div class="code-window__body">
<pre><span class="tok-cmt">[Basilisk Debug]</span> createDebugAdapterDescriptor
  type=<span class="tok-type">basilisk-debug</span>, request=<span class="tok-str">launch</span>

<span class="tok-cmt">[Basilisk Debug]</span> Requesting LSP to spawn
  debugpy (python: <span class="tok-fn">auto-detect</span>)...

<span class="tok-cmt">[Basilisk Debug]</span> LSP spawned debugpy &rarr;
  connecting DAP client to
  <span class="tok-type">localhost</span>:<span class="tok-num">57356</span></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-row__text">
      <p class="feature-row__num">05 &mdash; Integrated debugging</p>
      <h2 class="feature-row__title">F5 to debug.<br>Basilisk handles the rest.</h2>
      <p class="feature-row__body">
        Set breakpoints, step through code, inspect variables &mdash; all
        brokered through the Basilisk LSP. The Rust server spawns debugpy on a free
        TCP port and connects VS Code&rsquo;s DAP client automatically. No manual
        configuration. No separate debug extension. Just press F5.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-row">
    <div class="feature-row__text">
      <p class="feature-row__num">06 &mdash; Single binary</p>
      <h2 class="feature-row__title">Drop it in PATH.<br>That&rsquo;s the whole install.</h2>
      <p class="feature-row__body">
        Compiled to a standalone Rust binary. No Node.js. No Python interpreter.
        No <code>pip install</code>. No <code>npm install</code>.
        Works on macOS, Linux, and Windows from a single download.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-row__visual">
